^["intent"]{
 ~#goal: "Establish Obsidian as the fourth interaction target for Spw, alongside VS Code, IntelliJ, and Neovim. The plugin renders .spw syntax via a CM6 token-map adapter wrapping spw-seed's lexer, spawns the spw-lsp subprocess for editor intelligence, and surfaces Concepts + Workspace Atlas as ItemView leaf panels. Obsidian adds a dimension the other targets lack: vault-native file topology, where .spw surfaces cohabit with markdown notes in a user-owned, local-first knowledge graph. This also functions as a portfolio piece for a staff engineer application at Obsidian — demonstrating deep engagement with their plugin API, CM6 extension model, and manifesto alignment."
 ~#taste: "layering — clean interaction-target abstraction where syntax, LSP, and views are independently useful; containment — each subsystem owns its own lifecycle and DOM; naming — operator-physics vocabulary (valence pentad, sigil semantics) carries into Obsidian's rendering; portability — spw-seed as single source of syntax truth across all four targets"
 ~#manifesto_alignment: "yours (free plugin, open-source), durable (plain-text .spw files, no proprietary format), private (everything local, LSP subprocess on-device, no network calls), malleable (configurable LSP path, custom sigil colors, extensible views), independent (no cloud dependency, no telemetry)"
}

